// src/kernel/core/acpi/aml_walker.pdx — Wave 59 D5 (paideia-os#2522)
//
// Minimal ACPICA-compatible AML walker for DSDT + SSDT definition
// blocks. Decodes the *declaration-level* bytecode subset needed for
// driver bring-up (OpRegion handles, named integers / buffers, named
// methods, device scopes) and populates a fixed-size `AmlTable` of
// name-indexed entries. The walker DOES NOT execute methods — method
// bodies are recorded as raw bytecode slices (body_off + body_len)
// and the companion entrypoint `aml_method_eval` is a stub that
// returns 0 until the Wave 59 D5 follow-up slice lands a term-arg
// evaluator.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Architectural tension flag (read this before touching the file)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The project's `design/acpi/no-aml-in-kernel.md` + the two companion
// lints (`tools/lint-no-kernel-aml.sh` and `tools/verify-aml-parser.sh §1`)
// forbid AML identifiers / files under `src/kernel/**`, with the full
// AML pipeline living in `src/user/aml/*.pdx` (17 modules). This
// walker lives in-kernel BY EXPLICIT WAVE 59 D5 DIRECTIVE: driver
// bring-up (KIND_INTERRUPT chains, EC _REG arbitration, GPE/SCI wiring,
// PCI _ADR / _CRS resolution) needs the DSDT namespace index BEFORE
// the userspace `acpi_supervisor` process is spawnable, so a bounded,
// name-indexing-only walker ships kernel-side and the Turing-complete
// evaluator stays in userspace. The two lints have been given a
// targeted exemption for THIS file only; see the comment in each.
//
// The invariant that preserves the no-AML-in-kernel pillar's SPIRIT
// here is:  this walker NEVER EVALUATES method bodies. It parses the
// static declaration shape (ScopeOp / DeviceOp / MethodOp / NameOp /
// OpRegionOp / BufferOp) and nothing else; every method body is kept
// as an opaque bytecode slice for userspace evaluation. The one-slice
// opcode-decode surface is intentionally too small to express an
// arbitrary computation.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Design cross-refs
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   * `design/drivers/kind-interrupt.md` (Wave 60) — D1 KIND_INTERRUPT
//     chain. The _PRT / _CRS / _REG methods this walker INDEXES are
//     the firmware anchors a KIND_INTERRUPT MINT consults when
//     building vector → endpoint routes.
//   * `design/acpi/aml-parser.md` — the full userspace AML tokenizer /
//     namespace parser / term-evaluator pipeline. THIS walker is the
//     kernel's narrow early-boot subset of that pipeline; the two must
//     stay semantically consistent on the opcode subset they share.
//   * `design/acpi/no-aml-in-kernel.md` — the firewall this file
//     deliberately crosses, with the method-eval-stays-userspace
//     invariant above as the mitigation.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// On-wire format (ACPI 6.5 §19.2, §20.2)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A DSDT / SSDT is an SDT header (36 B — caller strips it) followed
// by a TermList: a flat sequence of TermObj.
//
// Each TermObj starts with a one-byte opcode (or a 0x5B ExtOpPrefix
// then a one-byte extended opcode).  The declaration-level opcodes
// this walker DISPATCHES on:
//
//   0x08          NameOp        NameString DataRefObject
//   0x10          ScopeOp       PkgLength NameString TermList
//   0x11          BufferOp      PkgLength BufferSize ByteList
//                               (only reached as the RHS of NameOp)
//   0x14          MethodOp      PkgLength NameString MethodFlags TermList
//   0x5B 0x80     OpRegionOp    NameString RegionSpace RegionOffset
//                               RegionLength
//   0x5B 0x82     DeviceOp      PkgLength NameString TermList
//
// Every other opcode (0xA0 IfOp, 0xA1 ElseOp, 0xA2 WhileOp, 0xA4
// ReturnOp, 0x72 AddOp, 0x74 SubOp, 0x79 ShiftLeft, 0x7A ShiftRight,
// 0x7B AndOp, 0x7D OrOp, 0x99 ToIntegerOp, 0x5B 0x23 AcqOp, 0x5B
// 0x27 ReleaseOp, …) is a METHOD-BODY opcode and this walker never
// sees it in dispatch position: Method bodies are skipped en bloc via
// their enclosing PkgLength. The constants are declared below so the
// follow-up method evaluator finds them in a predictable place; the
// walker itself does not act on them.
//
// PkgLength encoding (ACPI 6.5 §20.2.4):
//   Byte 0 bits [7:6] = follow-byte count N (0..3).
//   N == 0 : value = byte0 & 0x3F.                  Total 1 byte.
//   N >= 1 : value = (byte0 & 0x0F)
//                  | (byte1 << 4)
//                  | (byte2 << 12 if N >= 2)
//                  | (byte3 << 20 if N == 3).       Total 1+N bytes.
//   The VALUE represents the total byte-count of the package
//   including the PkgLength bytes themselves, NOT including the
//   preceding opcode byte(s). Therefore pkg_end offset = (position
//   of PkgLength byte 0) + value.
//
// NameString encoding (ACPI 6.5 §20.2.2):
//   An optional prefix sequence followed by exactly one NamePath.
//   Prefix: 0x5C RootChar (at most once at the very start) and/or
//           any number of 0x5E ParentPrefixChar.
//   NamePath :=
//     0x00                                NullName
//     NameSeg                             single 4-byte name (ASCII)
//     0x2E NameSeg NameSeg                DualNamePath (2 segments)
//     0x2F SegCount:u8 (NameSeg * SegCount)   MultiNamePath
//
// This walker stores the *deepest* NameSeg per declaration (the last
// 4 ASCII bytes of the NamePath), which is sufficient for the
// name-based lookups the Wave 59 D5 consumers need (EC@_HID, LPCB,
// PCI0, _CRS, _REG, _PRT, …). A follow-up slice may extend AmlTable
// entries with a parent-scope index pointer to materialise the full
// namespace path.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AmlTable layout (7456 B total, @align(8))
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Header (32 B):
//     +0   u32  n_devices
//     +4   u32  n_methods
//     +8   u32  n_opregions
//     +12  u32  n_integers
//     +16  u32  n_buffers
//     +20  u32  flags           bit0 = malformed_detected
//     +24  u64  scratch_value   helper write-slot (term-arg int decode)
//
//   Devices  : offset 32,   32 × 32 B = 1024 B
//     +0   u32  name_seg
//     +4   u32  _pad
//     +8   u64  decl_off       byte offset of the ScopeOp opcode from dsdt base
//     +16  u64  pkg_end_off    byte offset of the first byte past this device
//     +24  u64  _reserved
//
//   Methods  : offset 1056, 128 × 32 B = 4096 B
//     +0   u32  name_seg
//     +4   u32  method_flags   ArgCount (bits 2..0), SerializeFlag (bit 3),
//                              SyncLevel (bits 7..4)
//     +8   u64  body_off       byte offset of first byte of TermList
//     +16  u64  body_len       length of the method's TermList in bytes
//     +24  u64  _reserved
//
//   OpRegions: offset 5152, 16 × 32 B = 512 B
//     +0   u32  name_seg
//     +4   u32  address_space  0=SysMem, 1=SysIO, 2=PCI_Cfg, 3=EC,
//                              4=SMBus, 5=CMOS, 6=PCI_BAR, ...
//     +8   u64  region_offset  decoded from the OpRegion's offset TermArg
//     +16  u64  region_length  decoded from the length TermArg
//     +24  u64  _reserved
//
//   Integers: offset 5664, 64 × 16 B = 1024 B
//     +0   u32  name_seg
//     +4   u32  width_bytes    1 (byte) | 2 (word) | 4 (dword) | 8 (qword)
//     +8   u64  value
//
//   Buffers:  offset 6688, 32 × 24 B = 768 B
//     +0   u32  name_seg
//     +4   u32  _pad
//     +8   u64  buf_off        offset of first ByteData from dsdt base
//     +16  u64  buf_len        declared BufferSize integer value
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// paideia-as encoder-gap posture (v0.36.81)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   G1 (`mov [mem], reg32` widens): every u32 store (count fields,
//      flags, device/method/opregion/integer/buffer header slots)
//      uses `mov_d`.
//   G4 (`cmp al, imm` may miscompile): every compare in this file is
//      against a full 64-bit register, no `cmp al,imm`.
//   Reserved labels: `loop` / `if` / `let` cannot be labels. Every
//      label in this file is `walk_` / `pkln_` / `ns_` / `arg_` /
//      `me_`-prefixed per discipline. The module name is `AmlWalker`
//      not `Aml` so no identifier starts with the reserved `let`.
//   Unaligned reads: the DSDT bytecode starts at an arbitrary byte
//      offset in the SDT (36 B past the header), so every multi-byte
//      load is treated as unaligned per SDM Vol.3 §7.1.1 (same posture
//      as `crs.pdx` L109 and `mcfg.pdx` L203). u8 via `xor + mov_b`,
//      u16 via `xor + mov_w`, u32 via `xor + mov r32,[...]` (the 32-
//      bit MOV zero-extends into the full 64-bit register per SDM
//      Vol.1 §3.4.1.1), u64 via plain `mov r64,[...]`.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Register plan (aml_walk_dsdt)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   SysV: (dsdt_phys, dsdt_len, out_table) → status (1=OK, 0=malformed).
//   Prologue: push rbx, r12, r13, r14, r15 (5 pushes → rsp%16==0).
//     r12 = dsdt_base (preserved; needed for computing decl offsets)
//     r13 = end = dsdt_base + dsdt_len
//     r14 = cursor
//     r15 = out_table (AmlTable base)
//     rbx = scratch for carrying decode results across helper calls

module AmlWalker = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // AML opcode constants (ACPI 6.5 §20.2.5)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Declaration-level opcodes this walker dispatches on:
  pub let AML_OP_ZERO            : u64 = 0x00
  pub let AML_OP_ONE             : u64 = 0x01
  pub let AML_OP_NAME            : u64 = 0x08
  pub let AML_OP_BYTE_PREFIX     : u64 = 0x0A
  pub let AML_OP_WORD_PREFIX     : u64 = 0x0B
  pub let AML_OP_DWORD_PREFIX    : u64 = 0x0C
  pub let AML_OP_STRING_PREFIX   : u64 = 0x0D
  pub let AML_OP_QWORD_PREFIX    : u64 = 0x0E
  pub let AML_OP_SCOPE           : u64 = 0x10
  pub let AML_OP_BUFFER          : u64 = 0x11
  pub let AML_OP_PACKAGE         : u64 = 0x12
  pub let AML_OP_VAR_PACKAGE     : u64 = 0x13
  pub let AML_OP_METHOD          : u64 = 0x14
  pub let AML_OP_EXT_PREFIX      : u64 = 0x5B
  pub let AML_OP_ONES            : u64 = 0xFF
  //
  // Extended opcodes (after 0x5B):
  pub let AML_EXT_OPREGION       : u64 = 0x80
  pub let AML_EXT_DEVICE         : u64 = 0x82
  //
  // Reserved for method-body evaluator (follow-up slice; the walker
  // itself never dispatches on these — Method bodies are skipped en
  // bloc through their PkgLength).
  pub let AML_OP_RETURN          : u64 = 0xA4
  pub let AML_OP_IF              : u64 = 0xA0
  pub let AML_OP_ELSE            : u64 = 0xA1
  pub let AML_OP_WHILE           : u64 = 0xA2
  pub let AML_OP_TO_INTEGER      : u64 = 0x99
  pub let AML_OP_ADD             : u64 = 0x72
  pub let AML_OP_SUB             : u64 = 0x74
  pub let AML_OP_SHIFT_LEFT      : u64 = 0x79
  pub let AML_OP_SHIFT_RIGHT     : u64 = 0x7A
  pub let AML_OP_AND             : u64 = 0x7B
  pub let AML_OP_OR              : u64 = 0x7D
  pub let AML_EXT_ACQUIRE        : u64 = 0x23
  pub let AML_EXT_RELEASE        : u64 = 0x27

  //
  // NameString prefix / path discriminator bytes:
  pub let AML_NS_ROOT_CHAR       : u64 = 0x5C
  pub let AML_NS_PARENT_PREFIX   : u64 = 0x5E
  pub let AML_NS_DUAL_PREFIX     : u64 = 0x2E
  pub let AML_NS_MULTI_PREFIX    : u64 = 0x2F
  pub let AML_NS_NULL_NAME       : u64 = 0x00

  // ==========================================================================
  // AmlTable capacity constants
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let AML_T_CAP_DEVICES      : u64 = 32
  pub let AML_T_CAP_METHODS      : u64 = 128
  pub let AML_T_CAP_OPREGIONS    : u64 = 16
  pub let AML_T_CAP_INTEGERS     : u64 = 64
  pub let AML_T_CAP_BUFFERS      : u64 = 32

  // ==========================================================================
  // AmlTable field offsets (bytes from the record base)
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let AML_T_OFF_N_DEVICES    : u64 = 0
  pub let AML_T_OFF_N_METHODS    : u64 = 4
  pub let AML_T_OFF_N_OPREGIONS  : u64 = 8
  pub let AML_T_OFF_N_INTEGERS   : u64 = 12
  pub let AML_T_OFF_N_BUFFERS    : u64 = 16
  pub let AML_T_OFF_FLAGS        : u64 = 20
  pub let AML_T_OFF_SCRATCH_VAL  : u64 = 24

  pub let AML_T_OFF_DEVICES      : u64 = 32
  pub let AML_T_OFF_METHODS      : u64 = 1056
  pub let AML_T_OFF_OPREGIONS    : u64 = 5152
  pub let AML_T_OFF_INTEGERS     : u64 = 5664
  pub let AML_T_OFF_BUFFERS      : u64 = 6688

  pub let AML_T_SZ_DEVICE_ENTRY  : u64 = 32
  pub let AML_T_SZ_METHOD_ENTRY  : u64 = 32
  pub let AML_T_SZ_OPREGION_ENTRY: u64 = 32
  pub let AML_T_SZ_INTEGER_ENTRY : u64 = 16
  pub let AML_T_SZ_BUFFER_ENTRY  : u64 = 24

  pub let AML_T_SIZE             : u64 = 7456

  // Malformed flag bit (set in header +20 when a decoder bails).
  pub let AML_T_FLAG_MALFORMED   : u64 = 1

  // ==========================================================================
  // OpRegion address-space IDs (ACPI 6.5 §5.5.2.4)
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let AML_REGION_SYS_MEM     : u64 = 0x00
  pub let AML_REGION_SYS_IO      : u64 = 0x01
  pub let AML_REGION_PCI_CFG     : u64 = 0x02
  pub let AML_REGION_EC          : u64 = 0x03
  pub let AML_REGION_SMBUS       : u64 = 0x04
  pub let AML_REGION_CMOS        : u64 = 0x05
  pub let AML_REGION_PCI_BAR     : u64 = 0x06

  // ==========================================================================
  // Error sentinel for packed-return helpers
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // The three decode helpers (pkglen, namestring, termarg_int) pack
  // their small return values into one u64 so no stack buffer is
  // needed. Bit 63 is the error flag; a return with bit 63 set means
  // "cursor overshot, abandon walk". 0x8000000000000000 is the chosen
  // sentinel value — all helpers return it on error, zeroing every
  // other bit so the caller can bit-test with `test rax, rax` style
  // sign checks or `shr rax, 63`.

  pub let AML_HELPER_ERR_BIT     : u64 = 0x8000000000000000

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_walker_pkglen — PkgLength decoder
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // SysV: (cursor: u64, end: u64) -> u64
  //
  // Returns a packed value in rax:
  //   bits [31:0]  = PkgLength value (ACPI 6.5 §20.2.4). Values do not
  //                  exceed 2^28 - 1 by construction (max 4-byte PkgLength),
  //                  so 32 bits is 16× headroom.
  //   bits [39:32] = bytes-consumed count (1, 2, 3 or 4). Fits in 8 bits.
  //   bit  [63]    = error flag (1 = malformed / cursor overshot).
  //
  // Leaf function — uses only caller-save registers (rax, rcx, rdx,
  // r8, r9, r10, r11) so the caller does not need to spill rbx /
  // r12-r15 across the call.

  let aml_walker_pkglen : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (cursor: u64, end: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: { boot },
      justification: "Wave 59 D5 (paideia-os#2522). ACPI 6.5 §20.2.4 PkgLength decoder. Leaf function: no push, no call. rdi=cursor, rsi=end. (1) Bounds-check cursor < end (need at least the lead byte); else return AML_HELPER_ERR_BIT. (2) Load lead byte via xor+mov_b (G4 — full-register compare, SDM Vol.1 §3.4.1.1 zero-extends via xor). (3) Compute follow_bytes = (lead >> 6) & 0x3. (4) If follow_bytes==0: value = lead & 0x3F, bytes_consumed = 1; pack and return. (5) Else: bounds-check cursor + 1 + follow_bytes <= end; value = (lead & 0x0F); then for each follow byte i in 1..=follow_bytes, OR in (byte_i << (4 + 8*(i-1))) via xor+mov_b. (6) Pack: rax = value | ((follow_bytes + 1) << 32); return. Labels prefixed pkln_ to avoid reserved loop/if/let. Every compare is on a full 64-bit register (G4); the single memory store would be a store (there is none here — pure arith) so G1 does not apply.",
      block: {
        // Bounds-check: cursor < end (need at least 1 byte).
        cmp rdi, rsi;
        jae pkln_err;

        // Load lead byte.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdi];

        // follow_bytes = (lead >> 6) & 0x3
        mov rcx, rax;
        shr rcx, 6;
        and rcx, 3;

        cmp rcx, 0;
        jne pkln_multi;

        // Single-byte PkgLength: value = lead & 0x3F; bytes = 1.
        mov rdx, rax;
        and rdx, 0x3F;
        mov rax, 1;
        shl rax, 32;
        or rax, rdx;
        ret;

      pkln_multi:
        // total_bytes = 1 + follow_bytes. Need cursor + total_bytes <= end.
        mov r8, rcx;
        add r8, 1;
        mov r9, rdi;
        add r9, r8;
        cmp r9, rsi;
        ja pkln_err;

        // value = lead & 0x0F
        mov rdx, rax;
        and rdx, 0x0F;

        // byte1 << 4
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdi + 1];
        shl rax, 4;
        or rdx, rax;

        cmp rcx, 1;
        je pkln_pack;

        // byte2 << 12
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdi + 2];
        shl rax, 12;
        or rdx, rax;

        cmp rcx, 2;
        je pkln_pack;

        // byte3 << 20
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdi + 3];
        shl rax, 20;
        or rdx, rax;

      pkln_pack:
        // rax = (bytes_consumed << 32) | value_lo_32
        mov rax, rcx;
        add rax, 1;
        shl rax, 32;
        or rax, rdx;
        ret;

      pkln_err:
        mov rax, 0x8000000000000000;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_walker_namestring — NameString decoder
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // SysV: (cursor: u64, end: u64) -> u64
  //
  // Returns a packed value in rax:
  //   bits [31:0]  = deepest NameSeg as a u32 (4 ASCII chars, little-
  //                  endian). 0 for NullName.
  //   bits [55:32] = bytes-consumed count (fits a few hundred bytes
  //                  worth of multi-seg paths).
  //   bit  [63]    = error flag.
  //
  // Leaf function — only caller-save registers used.

  let aml_walker_namestring : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (cursor: u64, end: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: { boot },
      justification: "Wave 59 D5 (paideia-os#2522). ACPI 6.5 §20.2.2 NameString decoder. Leaf function: no push, no call. rdi=cursor, rsi=end. r8 tracks bytes_consumed across the prefix loop and the namepath branch. r9 tracks the moving cursor (we never mutate rdi — the caller may want to inspect the entry byte). (1) Prefix loop: consume any number of 0x5C (RootChar) or 0x5E (ParentPrefixChar) bytes, incrementing r8 and r9. (2) Namepath discriminator read as u8 via xor+mov_b. (3) Dispatch: 0x00 → NullName (name=0, +1 byte). 0x2E → DualNamePath (name = second NameSeg, +9 bytes: 1 prefix + 2*4 segs). 0x2F → MultiNamePath (name = last NameSeg; total = 2 + SegCount*4 bytes; SegCount read via xor+mov_b). Otherwise → single NameSeg at r9 (4 bytes). (4) Every branch bounds-checks (cursor + size <= end) before loading the NameSeg (G4 — full-register compares). u32 NameSeg loads use `mov r10d, [...]` which zero-extends per SDM Vol.1 §3.4.1.1. (5) Pack rax = (bytes_consumed << 32) | nameseg_u32; return. Error → 0x8000000000000000. Labels prefixed ns_ to avoid reserved loop/if/let.",
      block: {
        xor r8, r8;                    // bytes_consumed = 0
        mov r9, rdi;                   // moving cursor
        xor r10, r10;                  // last NameSeg accumulator

      ns_prefix_top:
        cmp r9, rsi;
        jae ns_err;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r9];
        cmp rax, 0x5C;                 // RootChar
        je ns_prefix_adv;
        cmp rax, 0x5E;                 // ParentPrefixChar
        je ns_prefix_adv;
        jmp ns_dispatch;

      ns_prefix_adv:
        add r9, 1;
        add r8, 1;
        jmp ns_prefix_top;

      ns_dispatch:
        // rax already holds the discriminator byte.
        cmp rax, 0x00;
        je ns_null;
        cmp rax, 0x2E;
        je ns_dual;
        cmp rax, 0x2F;
        je ns_multi;

        // Single NameSeg: 4 bytes starting at r9.
        mov r11, r9;
        add r11, 4;
        cmp r11, rsi;
        ja ns_err;
        xor r10, r10;
        mov r10d, [r9];                // zero-extends u32 → r10
        add r9, 4;
        add r8, 4;
        jmp ns_pack;

      ns_null:
        xor r10, r10;
        add r9, 1;
        add r8, 1;
        jmp ns_pack;

      ns_dual:
        // 1 prefix byte + 2 NameSegs = 9 bytes.
        mov r11, r9;
        add r11, 9;
        cmp r11, rsi;
        ja ns_err;
        // last_nameseg = *(u32*)(r9 + 1 + 4) = *(u32*)(r9 + 5).
        xor r10, r10;
        mov r10d, [r9 + 5];
        add r9, 9;
        add r8, 9;
        jmp ns_pack;

      ns_multi:
        // Need prefix + SegCount byte = 2 bytes available first.
        mov r11, r9;
        add r11, 2;
        cmp r11, rsi;
        ja ns_err;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r9 + 1];           // SegCount

        // total_bytes = 2 + SegCount*4
        mov rcx, rax;
        shl rcx, 2;
        mov r11, r9;
        add r11, 2;
        add r11, rcx;
        cmp r11, rsi;
        ja ns_err;

        cmp rax, 0;
        je ns_multi_zero;

        // last_nameseg = *(u32*)(r9 + 2 + (SegCount - 1) * 4)
        mov rdx, rax;
        sub rdx, 1;
        shl rdx, 2;
        add rdx, 2;
        add rdx, r9;
        xor r10, r10;
        mov r10d, [rdx];
        jmp ns_multi_commit;

      ns_multi_zero:
        xor r10, r10;

      ns_multi_commit:
        // advance by (2 + SegCount*4) = 2 + rcx
        mov r11, rcx;
        add r11, 2;
        add r9, r11;
        add r8, r11;

      ns_pack:
        // rax = (r8 << 32) | r10
        mov rax, r8;
        shl rax, 32;
        or rax, r10;
        ret;

      ns_err:
        mov rax, 0x8000000000000000;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_walker_termarg_int — integer-constant TermArg decoder
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // SysV: (cursor: u64, end: u64, out_table: u64) -> u64
  //
  // Decodes a TermArg that must resolve to an integer constant: one
  // of 0x00 (Zero), 0x01 (One), 0xFF (Ones), 0x0A/0x0B/0x0C/0x0E
  // (ByteConst / WordConst / DWordConst / QWordConst). Any other
  // leading byte is an unsupported TermArg form (e.g. a method
  // invocation, a reference, an arithmetic expression) and we return
  // AML_HELPER_ERR_BIT: the Wave 59 D5 first slice decodes only the
  // declaration-level constants used by OpRegion offset/length and
  // Name(_, <integer>) values.
  //
  // Writes the decoded value as a u64 to [out_table + AML_T_OFF_SCRATCH_VAL]
  // (i.e. AmlTable+24). The caller reads that slot after a successful
  // call and before issuing another helper call. Returns in rax a
  // packed:
  //   bits [31:0] = bytes-consumed
  //   bit  [63]   = error flag

  let aml_walker_termarg_int : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (cursor: u64, end: u64, out_table: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: { boot },
      justification: "Wave 59 D5 (paideia-os#2522). ACPI 6.5 §20.2.3 integer-constant TermArg decoder. Leaf. rdi=cursor, rsi=end, rdx=out_table. (1) Bounds-check cursor < end for the lead byte; else error. (2) Read lead via xor+mov_b. (3) Dispatch: 0x00 Zero → value=0, bytes=1. 0x01 One → value=1, bytes=1. 0xFF Ones → value=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, bytes=1. 0x0A ByteConst → value=u8@+1, bytes=2 (need +2 bounds). 0x0B WordConst → value=u16@+1, bytes=3 (unaligned mov_w via xor+mov_w). 0x0C DWordConst → value=u32@+1, bytes=5 (mov r32d,[...] zero-extends per SDM §3.4.1.1). 0x0E QWordConst → value=u64@+1, bytes=9 (plain mov r64,[...] — unaligned OK per SDM Vol.3 §7.1.1). (4) Store value to [rdx + 24] via plain mov (u64 slot — no mov_d needed). (5) Pack rax = bytes_consumed; return. Any other lead byte → AML_HELPER_ERR_BIT. Labels prefixed arg_ per reserved-label discipline. Full-register compares (G4). u32 stores not applicable here (the write is a u64).",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, rsi;
        jae arg_err;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdi];

        // 0x00 Zero
        cmp rax, 0x00;
        jne arg_check_one;
        xor r8, r8;
        mov [rdx + 24], r8;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;

      arg_check_one:
        cmp rax, 0x01;
        jne arg_check_ones;
        mov r8, 1;
        mov [rdx + 24], r8;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;

      arg_check_ones:
        cmp rax, 0xFF;
        jne arg_check_byte;
        mov r8, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        mov [rdx + 24], r8;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;

      arg_check_byte:
        cmp rax, 0x0A;
        jne arg_check_word;
        // need cursor+2 bytes
        mov r9, rdi;
        add r9, 2;
        cmp r9, rsi;
        ja arg_err;
        xor r8, r8;
        mov_b r8, [rdi + 1];
        mov [rdx + 24], r8;
        mov rax, 2;
        ret;

      arg_check_word:
        cmp rax, 0x0B;
        jne arg_check_dword;
        mov r9, rdi;
        add r9, 3;
        cmp r9, rsi;
        ja arg_err;
        xor r8, r8;
        mov_w r8, [rdi + 1];
        mov [rdx + 24], r8;
        mov rax, 3;
        ret;

      arg_check_dword:
        cmp rax, 0x0C;
        jne arg_check_qword;
        mov r9, rdi;
        add r9, 5;
        cmp r9, rsi;
        ja arg_err;
        xor r8, r8;
        mov r8d, [rdi + 1];
        mov [rdx + 24], r8;
        mov rax, 5;
        ret;

      arg_check_qword:
        cmp rax, 0x0E;
        jne arg_err;
        mov r9, rdi;
        add r9, 9;
        cmp r9, rsi;
        ja arg_err;
        mov r8, [rdi + 1];             // u64 load (unaligned OK)
        mov [rdx + 24], r8;
        mov rax, 9;
        ret;

      arg_err:
        mov rax, 0x8000000000000000;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_walk_dsdt — the top-level AML name-index walker
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // SysV: (dsdt_phys: u64, dsdt_len: u64, out_table: u64) -> u64
  //
  //   rdi = pointer to the DSDT / SSDT bytecode (caller strips the 36 B
  //         SDT header so this is the first byte of the TermList).
  //   rsi = length of the bytecode in bytes (caller passes dsdt_len
  //         zero-extended into a full u64).
  //   rdx = pointer to a caller-allocated AmlTable (AML_T_SIZE bytes,
  //         @align(8)). This routine zero-initialises the 32-byte
  //         header before walking; it does NOT zero the entry arrays
  //         (the count fields determine liveness, so unseeded tail
  //         entries are inert).
  //
  // Returns rax = 1 on success (walker reached end-of-buffer cleanly),
  //         rax = 0 on malformed (a decoder bailed; `flags` has
  //                               AML_T_FLAG_MALFORMED set in that case).
  //
  // Walk discipline — linear forward scan over the TermList:
  //
  //   * ScopeOp (0x10) / DeviceOp (0x5B 0x82):   DO NOT skip past the
  //       pkg_end. Advance only past (opcode + PkgLength + NameString)
  //       so the walker enters the enclosed TermList and continues
  //       finding inner declarations. For DeviceOp we record a device
  //       entry (name + decl_off + pkg_end_off); for ScopeOp we also
  //       record a device entry (the Scope keyword is how firmware
  //       re-opens existing namespaces like `\_SB` or `\_PR` — treating
  //       both uniformly keeps the index symmetric for namespace-
  //       walking consumers).
  //
  //   * MethodOp (0x14):                         DO skip past pkg_end.
  //       Record a method entry (name + method_flags + body_off +
  //       body_len) and jump to pkg_end so inner method-body opcodes
  //       are never seen by the dispatch.
  //
  //   * NameOp (0x08):                           DO consume the full
  //       object. Record the data as an integer or buffer entry
  //       depending on the DataRefObject's prefix byte.
  //
  //   * OpRegionOp (0x5B 0x80):                  DO consume the full
  //       object. Record an opregion entry (name + address_space +
  //       region_offset + region_length).
  //
  //   * Any other byte at dispatch position:     Advance 1 byte and
  //       continue. The declaration subset here is a strict subset of
  //       AML — a well-formed DSDT in dispatch position after the
  //       Scope/Device/Method/Name/OpRegion handlers advance past
  //       their own forms should never land here. If it does we are
  //       either (a) inside an unexpected opcode form we chose not to
  //       dispatch on (fine — the next TermObj boundary is just a few
  //       bytes further) or (b) misaligned on a prior bad decode (the
  //       malformed flag was already set and we are trying to limp to
  //       the end). Byte-at-a-time advancement is the smallest step
  //       that converges either way.

  pub let aml_walk_dsdt : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (dsdt_phys: u64, dsdt_len: u64, out_table: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: { boot },
      justification: "Wave 59 D5 (paideia-os#2522). Minimal AML declaration-level walker for DSDT / SSDT definition blocks. 5-push callee-save prologue (rbx, r12, r13, r14, r15) → rsp%16==0 at every call site; r12=dsdt_base preserved for decl-offset arithmetic, r13=end, r14=cursor, r15=out_table, rbx=per-iteration scratch. Zero-initialises the 32-byte AmlTable header via four u64 stores. Main loop at walk_top: cursor-vs-end bounds-check (jae walk_success); one-byte opcode load via xor+mov_b; dispatch to walk_scope / walk_method / walk_name / walk_ext (ExtOpPrefix 0x5B) by full-register compares (G4). Unknown opcodes advance 1 byte and continue. walk_scope: advance past opcode byte, call aml_walker_pkglen to decode PkgLength value+bytes; record pkg_end_abs_off = (cursor_at_pkglen_start - r12) + pkglen_value; advance cursor past the PkgLength bytes; call aml_walker_namestring; store a device entry (name + decl_off + pkg_end_off) if n_devices < CAP; advance cursor past the NameString and continue (entering the TermList inline). walk_method: like walk_scope but the record goes in the methods table (name + method_flags u8 at cursor-after-namestring + body_off = cursor-after-flags + body_len = pkg_end - body_off); AFTER recording, we JUMP cursor to pkg_end so method body opcodes are never dispatched. walk_name: advance past NameOp, call aml_walker_namestring, then peek the DataRefObject prefix byte. If 0x11 (BufferOp): decode as a named buffer (PkgLength, BufferSize TermArg via aml_walker_termarg_int, buf_off, buf_len; advance to pkg_end). Else: call aml_walker_termarg_int; if ok, record as integer entry (width = 1/2/4/8 depending on prefix: 0x0A→1, 0x0B→2, 0x0C→4, 0x0E→8, 0x00/0x01/0xFF→1); advance past the term-arg bytes. If termarg_int fails (unsupported TermArg form), set malformed flag + bail. walk_ext: load next byte via xor+mov_b; if 0x82 → walk_device (identical to walk_scope but entry recorded with Device flag — present-slot flag TBD); if 0x80 → walk_opregion (advance past 0x5B 0x80; namestring; read 1 byte RegionSpace; two termarg_int calls for offset + length; record opregion entry); otherwise advance 2 bytes. All compares are 64-bit full-register (G4). All u32 stores (count field bumps, method_flags, address_space, width) use mov_d (G1). Unaligned reads treated per SDM Vol.3 §7.1.1 (xor+mov_b for u8, xor+mov_w for u16, xor+mov r32d for u32, plain mov r64 for u64). Reserved-label discipline: every label walk_/me_/dv_/op_/nm_-prefixed; module name AmlWalker not Aml so no identifier starts with reserved let/if/loop. On any decoder error (helper returned bit63 set, or a cap-overflow, or an inconsistent pkg_end) we set AML_T_FLAG_MALFORMED at out+20 via mov_d and bail to walk_fail. Capacity overflows (n_devices >= AML_T_CAP_DEVICES etc.) are NOT a hard error — the entry is silently dropped (we still advance past it) so firmware with more declarations than our caps simply yields a truncated index, not a bail. The count fields do NOT exceed the caps even in that case: we check cap-before-increment on every bump.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;                  // dsdt base
        mov r14, rdi;                  // cursor
        mov r13, rdi;
        add r13, rsi;                  // end = base + len
        mov r15, rdx;                  // out table

        // Zero-init the 32-byte header (4 × u64).
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r15 +  0], rax;
        mov [r15 +  8], rax;
        mov [r15 + 16], rax;
        mov [r15 + 24], rax;

      walk_top:
        cmp r14, r13;
        jae walk_success;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r14];

        cmp rax, 0x10;
        je walk_scope;
        cmp rax, 0x14;
        je walk_method;
        cmp rax, 0x08;
        je walk_name;
        cmp rax, 0x5B;
        je walk_ext;

        // Unknown dispatch byte: advance 1 and retry.
        add r14, 1;
        jmp walk_top;

        // ============================================================
        // walk_scope — ScopeOp (0x10) PkgLength NameString TermList
        //
        //   The walker records a device-table entry whose decl_off is
        //   the offset of the ScopeOp byte itself and whose pkg_end_off
        //   is the end of the enclosed TermList. Cursor is left AFTER
        //   the NameString so the loop enters the inner TermList on
        //   the next iteration.
        // ============================================================
      walk_scope:
        // Save the decl_off (r14 - r12 for this iteration).
        mov rbx, r14;
        sub rbx, r12;                  // rbx = decl_off (we'll use after helper calls)

        add r14, 1;                    // past ScopeOp byte

        // Decode PkgLength starting at r14.
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_walker_pkglen;

        // Error check: bit 63.
        mov r11, rax;
        shr r11, 63;
        cmp r11, 1;
        je walk_fail;

        // r10 = pkg_len_value (low 32 bits)
        mov r10, rax;
        and r10, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        // r9 = pkg_len_bytes ((rax >> 32) & 0xFF)
        mov r9, rax;
        shr r9, 32;
        and r9, 0xFF;

        // pkg_end_abs = r14 (cursor at PkgLength byte 0) + r10.
        mov r8, r14;
        add r8, r10;                   // r8 = pkg_end absolute pointer
        cmp r8, r13;
        ja walk_malformed;

        // Advance cursor past PkgLength bytes.
        add r14, r9;

        // Decode NameString starting at r14.
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_walker_namestring;

        mov r11, rax;
        shr r11, 63;
        cmp r11, 1;
        je walk_fail;

        // r10 = NameSeg (low 32)
        mov r10, rax;
        and r10, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        // r9 = bytes_consumed ((rax >> 32) & 0xFFFFFF)
        mov r9, rax;
        shr r9, 32;
        and r9, 0xFFFFFF;

        // Record device entry if capacity available.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov eax, [r15 + 0];            // n_devices (u32)
        cmp rax, 32;                   // AML_T_CAP_DEVICES
        jae walk_scope_skip_record;

        // slot_off = n * 32
        mov rcx, rax;
        shl rcx, 5;
        // base of devices array = r15 + 32
        mov rdi, r15;
        add rdi, 32;
        add rdi, rcx;                  // rdi = &devices[n]

        // devices[n].name_seg = r10 (u32 store).
        mov_d [rdi + 0], r10;

        // devices[n].decl_off = rbx (u64).
        mov [rdi + 8], rbx;

        // devices[n].pkg_end_off = (r8 - r12).
        mov r11, r8;
        sub r11, r12;
        mov [rdi + 16], r11;

        // n_devices += 1 (G1 u32 store).
        add rax, 1;
        mov_d [r15 + 0], rax;

      walk_scope_skip_record:
        // Advance cursor past the NameString; enter the inner TermList.
        add r14, r9;
        jmp walk_top;

        // ============================================================
        // walk_method — MethodOp (0x14) PkgLength NameString
        //                            MethodFlags TermList
        //
        //   Records a method entry and SKIPS cursor to pkg_end so
        //   body opcodes are not dispatched.
        // ============================================================
      walk_method:
        mov rbx, r14;
        sub rbx, r12;                  // decl_off (unused for method entry today;
                                       //           keeps register plan symmetric
                                       //           with walk_scope and reserves
                                       //           a slot for a future entry
                                       //           extension — see _reserved
                                       //           field at method entry +24).

        add r14, 1;                    // past MethodOp byte
        mov r11, r14;                  // r11 = pkglen-start absolute pointer

        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_walker_pkglen;

        mov rcx, rax;
        shr rcx, 63;
        cmp rcx, 1;
        je walk_fail;

        mov r10, rax;
        and r10, 0xFFFFFFFF;           // pkg_len_value
        mov r9, rax;
        shr r9, 32;
        and r9, 0xFF;                  // pkg_len_bytes

        // pkg_end_abs = r11 + r10
        mov r8, r11;
        add r8, r10;
        cmp r8, r13;
        ja walk_malformed;

        add r14, r9;                   // cursor past PkgLength

        // Decode NameString.
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_walker_namestring;

        mov rcx, rax;
        shr rcx, 63;
        cmp rcx, 1;
        je walk_fail;

        // name_seg = low 32
        mov rbx, rax;
        and rbx, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        // bytes_consumed = (rax >> 32) & 0xFFFFFF
        mov r9, rax;
        shr r9, 32;
        and r9, 0xFFFFFF;

        add r14, r9;                   // cursor past NameString

        // Need 1 more byte for MethodFlags.
        mov rcx, r14;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, r8;
        ja walk_malformed;             // flags byte must fit before pkg_end

        xor rcx, rcx;
        mov_b rcx, [r14];              // MethodFlags
        add r14, 1;                    // cursor past flags; now at body start

        // body_off = r14 - r12; body_len = r8 - r14.
        mov r10, r14;
        sub r10, r12;                  // body_off
        mov r11, r8;
        sub r11, r14;                  // body_len

        // Record method entry if capacity.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov eax, [r15 + 4];            // n_methods (u32)
        cmp rax, 128;                  // AML_T_CAP_METHODS
        jae walk_method_skip_record;

        mov rdx, rax;
        shl rdx, 5;                    // n*32
        mov rdi, r15;
        add rdi, 1056;                 // AML_T_OFF_METHODS
        add rdi, rdx;                  // rdi = &methods[n]

        mov_d [rdi + 0], rbx;          // name_seg (u32)
        mov_d [rdi + 4], rcx;          // method_flags (u32)
        mov   [rdi + 8], r10;          // body_off (u64)
        mov   [rdi + 16], r11;         // body_len (u64)

        add rax, 1;
        mov_d [r15 + 4], rax;          // n_methods += 1

      walk_method_skip_record:
        // Skip cursor to pkg_end — the method body is NOT walked.
        mov r14, r8;
        jmp walk_top;

        // ============================================================
        // walk_name — NameOp (0x08) NameString DataRefObject
        // ============================================================
      walk_name:
        add r14, 1;                    // past NameOp

        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_walker_namestring;

        mov r11, rax;
        shr r11, 63;
        cmp r11, 1;
        je walk_fail;

        // name_seg = low 32
        mov rbx, rax;
        and rbx, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        // bytes_consumed = (rax >> 32) & 0xFFFFFF
        mov r9, rax;
        shr r9, 32;
        and r9, 0xFFFFFF;

        add r14, r9;                   // cursor past NameString; at DataRefObject

        // Need at least 1 byte for the prefix.
        cmp r14, r13;
        jae walk_malformed;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r14];              // DataRefObject prefix byte

        // ---- BufferOp (0x11) → named buffer ----
        cmp rax, 0x11;
        je walk_name_buffer;

        // ---- PackageOp / VarPackageOp (0x12 / 0x13) → skip whole pkg ----
        // Skip strategy: advance past the prefix byte then decode its
        // PkgLength and jump cursor to pkg_end. We do NOT index the
        // package today (follow-up: a package-of-integers decoder for
        // _PRT entries).
        cmp rax, 0x12;
        je walk_name_pkg;
        cmp rax, 0x13;
        je walk_name_pkg;

        // ---- StringPrefix (0x0D) → skip null-terminated ----
        // Also unsupported in the first slice — advance over bytes
        // until a 0x00 terminator or end-of-buffer. We do not record
        // the string.
        cmp rax, 0x0D;
        je walk_name_string;

        // ---- Everything else: integer-constant TermArg ----
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        mov rdx, r15;
        call aml_walker_termarg_int;

        mov r11, rax;
        shr r11, 63;
        cmp r11, 1;
        je walk_name_termarg_fail;

        // r10 = bytes_consumed
        mov r10, rax;
        and r10, 0xFFFFFFFF;

        // Width from the prefix we loaded above (still in... wait,
        // termarg_int clobbered rax). We re-load the prefix from
        // [r14]. (The prefix byte is unchanged in memory.)
        xor rcx, rcx;
        mov_b rcx, [r14];

        // width = 1 by default; 0x0B→2, 0x0C→4, 0x0E→8.
        mov r9, 1;
        cmp rcx, 0x0B;
        jne walk_name_width_check_dword;
        mov r9, 2;
        jmp walk_name_width_done;
      walk_name_width_check_dword:
        cmp rcx, 0x0C;
        jne walk_name_width_check_qword;
        mov r9, 4;
        jmp walk_name_width_done;
      walk_name_width_check_qword:
        cmp rcx, 0x0E;
        jne walk_name_width_done;
        mov r9, 8;
      walk_name_width_done:

        // Record integer entry if capacity.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov eax, [r15 + 12];           // n_integers
        cmp rax, 64;                   // AML_T_CAP_INTEGERS
        jae walk_name_int_skip_record;

        mov rdx, rax;
        shl rdx, 4;                    // n*16
        mov rdi, r15;
        add rdi, 5664;                 // AML_T_OFF_INTEGERS
        add rdi, rdx;

        mov_d [rdi + 0], rbx;          // name_seg
        mov_d [rdi + 4], r9;           // width_bytes
        // Value sits at [r15 + 24] (scratch slot).
        mov r11, [r15 + 24];
        mov [rdi + 8], r11;

        add rax, 1;
        mov_d [r15 + 12], rax;         // n_integers += 1

      walk_name_int_skip_record:
        add r14, r10;                  // cursor past the TermArg
        jmp walk_top;

      walk_name_termarg_fail:
        // Set malformed flag and bail — we cannot safely advance
        // without knowing the TermArg's size.
        mov r11, 1;
        mov_d [r15 + 20], r11;
        jmp walk_fail;

      walk_name_buffer:
        // Decode: BufferOp (0x11) PkgLength BufferSize ByteList
        //   Our cursor is at the 0x11 opcode.
        add r14, 1;                    // past 0x11

        mov r11, r14;                  // pkglen-start
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_walker_pkglen;

        mov rcx, rax;
        shr rcx, 63;
        cmp rcx, 1;
        je walk_fail;

        mov r10, rax;
        and r10, 0xFFFFFFFF;           // pkglen value
        mov r9, rax;
        shr r9, 32;
        and r9, 0xFF;                  // pkglen bytes

        mov r8, r11;
        add r8, r10;                   // pkg_end_abs
        cmp r8, r13;
        ja walk_malformed;

        add r14, r9;                   // past PkgLength → BufferSize starts here

        // BufferSize = integer TermArg.
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        mov rdx, r15;
        call aml_walker_termarg_int;

        mov rcx, rax;
        shr rcx, 63;
        cmp rcx, 1;
        je walk_name_buf_bail;

        mov r9, rax;
        and r9, 0xFFFFFFFF;            // bytes consumed by BufferSize
        add r14, r9;                   // cursor past BufferSize; at ByteList

        // buf_len = scratch u64 at [r15+24].
        mov r10, [r15 + 24];
        // buf_off = r14 - r12.
        mov r11, r14;
        sub r11, r12;

        // Record buffer entry if capacity.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov eax, [r15 + 16];           // n_buffers
        cmp rax, 32;                   // AML_T_CAP_BUFFERS
        jae walk_name_buf_jump;

        mov rdx, rax;
        mov rcx, 24;
        imul rdx, rcx;                 // n * 24 (entry size)
        mov rdi, r15;
        add rdi, 6688;                 // AML_T_OFF_BUFFERS
        add rdi, rdx;

        mov_d [rdi + 0], rbx;          // name_seg
        mov   [rdi + 8], r11;          // buf_off
        mov   [rdi + 16], r10;         // buf_len

        add rax, 1;
        mov_d [r15 + 16], rax;

      walk_name_buf_jump:
        // Jump cursor past the entire buffer package (ByteList
        // included) regardless of whether we recorded.
        mov r14, r8;
        jmp walk_top;

      walk_name_buf_bail:
        mov r11, 1;
        mov_d [r15 + 20], r11;
        jmp walk_fail;

      walk_name_pkg:
        // Package / VarPackage: advance past prefix, decode PkgLength,
        // jump to pkg_end. We don't index packages in the first slice.
        add r14, 1;
        mov r11, r14;
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_walker_pkglen;

        mov rcx, rax;
        shr rcx, 63;
        cmp rcx, 1;
        je walk_fail;

        mov r10, rax;
        and r10, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        mov r8, r11;
        add r8, r10;
        cmp r8, r13;
        ja walk_malformed;

        mov r14, r8;
        jmp walk_top;

      walk_name_string:
        // Null-terminated string: scan until 0x00 or end.
        add r14, 1;                    // past the 0x0D prefix
      walk_name_string_scan:
        cmp r14, r13;
        jae walk_malformed;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r14];
        add r14, 1;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne walk_name_string_scan;
        jmp walk_top;

        // ============================================================
        // walk_ext — ExtOpPrefix (0x5B) secondary dispatch
        // ============================================================
      walk_ext:
        // Need 1 more byte for the extended opcode.
        mov rcx, r14;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, r13;
        jae walk_malformed;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r14 + 1];

        cmp rax, 0x82;                 // DeviceOp
        je walk_device;
        cmp rax, 0x80;                 // OpRegionOp
        je walk_opregion;

        // Unknown extended opcode: advance 2 bytes.
        add r14, 2;
        jmp walk_top;

        // ============================================================
        // walk_device — DeviceOp (0x5B 0x82) PkgLength NameString TermList
        //
        //   Shares the shape of walk_scope but records with a Device
        //   discriminator in the entry's _reserved field (future;
        //   today both Scope and Device write the same entry shape).
        // ============================================================
      walk_device:
        mov rbx, r14;
        sub rbx, r12;                  // decl_off at the 0x5B byte
        add r14, 2;                    // past 0x5B 0x82

        mov r11, r14;                  // pkglen-start
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_walker_pkglen;

        mov rcx, rax;
        shr rcx, 63;
        cmp rcx, 1;
        je walk_fail;

        mov r10, rax;
        and r10, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        mov r9, rax;
        shr r9, 32;
        and r9, 0xFF;

        mov r8, r11;
        add r8, r10;
        cmp r8, r13;
        ja walk_malformed;

        add r14, r9;                   // cursor past PkgLength

        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_walker_namestring;

        mov r11, rax;
        shr r11, 63;
        cmp r11, 1;
        je walk_fail;

        mov r10, rax;
        and r10, 0xFFFFFFFF;           // name_seg
        mov r9, rax;
        shr r9, 32;
        and r9, 0xFFFFFF;              // bytes_consumed

        // Record device entry if capacity.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov eax, [r15 + 0];
        cmp rax, 32;
        jae walk_device_skip_record;

        mov rcx, rax;
        shl rcx, 5;
        mov rdi, r15;
        add rdi, 32;                   // AML_T_OFF_DEVICES
        add rdi, rcx;

        mov_d [rdi + 0], r10;          // name_seg
        mov   [rdi + 8], rbx;          // decl_off (relative)
        mov r11, r8;
        sub r11, r12;
        mov   [rdi + 16], r11;         // pkg_end_off

        add rax, 1;
        mov_d [r15 + 0], rax;

      walk_device_skip_record:
        add r14, r9;
        jmp walk_top;

        // ============================================================
        // walk_opregion — OpRegionOp (0x5B 0x80) NameString RegionSpace
        //                              RegionOffset RegionLength
        // ============================================================
      walk_opregion:
        add r14, 2;                    // past 0x5B 0x80

        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_walker_namestring;

        mov r11, rax;
        shr r11, 63;
        cmp r11, 1;
        je walk_fail;

        mov rbx, rax;
        and rbx, 0xFFFFFFFF;           // name_seg
        mov r9, rax;
        shr r9, 32;
        and r9, 0xFFFFFF;              // bytes_consumed

        add r14, r9;                   // cursor past NameString

        // RegionSpace: 1 byte.
        cmp r14, r13;
        jae walk_malformed;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r14];              // address_space
        mov rcx, rax;                  // rcx holds it across termarg calls
        add r14, 1;

        // RegionOffset: integer TermArg → scratch slot.
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        mov rdx, r15;
        call aml_walker_termarg_int;

        mov r11, rax;
        shr r11, 63;
        cmp r11, 1;
        je walk_opregion_bail;

        mov r9, rax;
        and r9, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        add r14, r9;

        // Snapshot offset out of scratch before overwriting.
        mov r10, [r15 + 24];           // r10 = region_offset

        // RegionLength: integer TermArg.
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r13;
        mov rdx, r15;
        call aml_walker_termarg_int;

        mov r11, rax;
        shr r11, 63;
        cmp r11, 1;
        je walk_opregion_bail;

        mov r9, rax;
        and r9, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        add r14, r9;

        mov r8, [r15 + 24];            // r8 = region_length

        // Record opregion entry if capacity.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov eax, [r15 + 8];            // n_opregions
        cmp rax, 16;                   // AML_T_CAP_OPREGIONS
        jae walk_opregion_skip_record;

        mov rdx, rax;
        shl rdx, 5;                    // n*32
        mov rdi, r15;
        add rdi, 5152;                 // AML_T_OFF_OPREGIONS
        add rdi, rdx;

        mov_d [rdi + 0], rbx;          // name_seg
        mov_d [rdi + 4], rcx;          // address_space
        mov   [rdi + 8], r10;          // region_offset
        mov   [rdi + 16], r8;          // region_length

        add rax, 1;
        mov_d [r15 + 8], rax;

      walk_opregion_skip_record:
        jmp walk_top;

      walk_opregion_bail:
        mov r11, 1;
        mov_d [r15 + 20], r11;
        jmp walk_fail;

        // ============================================================
        // Terminals
        // ============================================================

      walk_malformed:
        mov r11, 1;
        mov_d [r15 + 20], r11;         // flags |= AML_T_FLAG_MALFORMED
      walk_fail:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      walk_success:
        mov rax, 1;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_method_eval — method-body evaluator (STUB)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // SysV: (table: u64, method_name_seg: u64, arg_pack: u64) -> u64
  //
  //   rdi = pointer to a populated AmlTable (as returned by aml_walk_dsdt).
  //   rsi = method NameSeg (u32 packed in the low bits of a u64).
  //   rdx = opaque arg-pack (follow-up: packed arg0..arg6).
  //
  // Returns 0 unconditionally in the Wave 59 D5 first slice.
  //
  // Deferred to the Wave 59 D5 follow-up slice:
  //   * Linear scan of the methods table for a matching name_seg.
  //   * Term-body evaluator covering ReturnOp / IfOp / ElseOp /
  //     WhileOp / ToIntegerOp / AddOp / SubOp / AndOp / OrOp /
  //     ShiftLeftOp / ShiftRightOp / AcqOp (Global Lock) / ReleaseOp.
  //   * Named-integer / named-buffer dereference through the walker's
  //     index.
  //   * OpRegion field access routed through the userspace region
  //     accessors (SystemMemory / SystemIO safe; PCI_Cfg / EC gated
  //     through the registered backends).
  //
  // The stub ensures callers can wire the evaluator entrypoint into
  // the driver bring-up path today; evaluations will begin returning
  // real results once the follow-up slice lands.

  pub let aml_method_eval : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (table: u64, method_name_seg: u64, arg_pack: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: { boot },
      justification: "Wave 59 D5 (paideia-os#2522) stub. Returns 0 unconditionally. The follow-up slice adds the term-body evaluator (ACPI 6.5 §19.6, §20.2.5 opcodes 0x72/0x74/0x79/0x7A/0x7B/0x7D/0x99/0xA0/0xA1/0xA2/0xA4 plus ExtOpPrefix 0x5B 0x23 Acquire and 0x5B 0x27 Release for the AML Global Lock). Not a leaf today but kept symmetric with the final shape so callers wire the entrypoint early; no pushes needed for a zero-returning stub. The three args are declared so the follow-up can switch to the real body without an ABI change; today they are referenced only to keep the parameter list meaningful in the elaborator. Full-register semantics only — nothing to compare, nothing to store. No G1 or G4 surface in a xor-rax-rax return.",
      block: {
        // Reference every arg once so the elaborator does not warn.
        // These moves are dead stores to caller-save registers and
        // the optimizer-equivalent produces identical machine code to
        // a bare `xor rax, rax; ret`.
        mov rax, rdi;
        mov rax, rsi;
        mov rax, rdx;
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }
}
